\title{Exploring the Limits of Machine-Learning Classification of Neutron-Star Matter Models:\\[2pt]
\large Populations of Equations of State, Illustrative Observational Errors and Calibrated Uncertainty}
\author{Wasif Husain\\ \small School of Science, Technology and Engineering, University of Sunshine Coast, Adelaide 5000}
\date{Revised manuscript (supersedes arXiv:2512.23758v3)}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Machine-learning classification of neutron-star composition is usually demonstrated on synthetic data in which every composition is a single equation of state (EoS) and test points are random draws along the same sequences.
We show first that the headline 97\% accuracy of a released benchmark of @@nTot@@ such models is largely interpolation between neighbouring models (the MLP accuracy falls from @@blksixMLP1@@\% to @@blksixMLP6@@\% and @@blksixMLP24@@\% when blocks of six and 24 models are withheld) and that its tabulated surface redshift leaks class information.
We then replace the benchmark by a population study of @@nEoS@@ EoS, @@nEoS0@@ in each of four composition scenarios (nucleonic, hyperonic-like, strange, and neutron-decay dark-matter-admixed), solved with a full general-relativistic TOV, tidal and $f$-mode code written for this work. The dark-matter family uses only dark-fermion masses inside the stability window ($m_\chi\le938.783$\,MeV); a wider prior is a sensitivity run. The code agrees to @@valFmax@@\% with six $f$-mode frequencies from an unpublished same-author pilot (computed with a third-party code) that shares the EoS inputs (a code-to-code consistency check, not an absolute accuracy), with analytic benchmarks (the Kelvin mode and Love number of an incompressible star, and the Schwarzschild quasi-normal mode), and gives damping times @@pubSixMin@@--@@pubSixMax@@\% above a published universal relation for nuclear EoS.
With whole-EoS hold-out, six classifiers (logistic regression, $k$-NN, random forest, gradient boosting, SVM and an MLP ensemble) reach only @@nf_MLP-ens_ALL@@\% accuracy even without measurement error (95\% bootstrap interval @@bs_ALL_0_MLP-ens_acc_lo@@--@@bs_ALL_0_MLP-ens_acc_hi@@\%, which resamples the EoS of the fixed catalogue and does not include regenerating the populations or retraining); the tested learners and a finite-reference kernel-Bayes classifier that uses the exact error model perform similarly.
Strange matter is separable (@@cm_ALL_0_11@@\% recall when $f$ is measured accurately), hyperonic-like matter partly (@@cm_ALL_0_33@@\%), but no discrimination between nucleonic and dark-matter-admixed matter is demonstrated in these experiments once the nuclear EoS is unknown (pairwise accuracy @@bs_ALL_0_MLP-ens_pair@@\%, interval @@bs_ALL_0_MLP-ens_pair_lo@@--@@bs_ALL_0_MLP-ens_pair_hi@@\%, which includes chance); with the nuclear EoS known exactly the dark component shifts $f$ by a median of @@pairDfMed@@\% and would need an $f$-mode precision of about @@pairSf_10_75@@\% (interval @@pairSf_10_75_lo@@--@@pairSf_10_75_hi@@\%) to be detected at 75\%. This shift depends on how baryon number is assigned to the one-fluid barotrope: changing the normalisation $m_0$ to 938.9\,MeV changes the shift of an individual EoS by a median of @@presRelMed@@\% and at most @@presRelMax@@\% inside the stability window, but by up to @@presWideOutMax@@\% for dark-fermion masses above it.
With illustrative errors taken from different published measurements and forecasts (NICER-like 10\% in $M$ and 9\% in $R$, and a 20\% $f$-mode measurement, which are not a realistic joint observation of one star) the accuracy is @@sc_NICER_mlp@@--@@sc_NICERpf20_mlp@@\% (chance 25\%) and conformal prediction sets contain @@pu_NICERpf20_t_setsize@@ of four classes; 5\%, 5\% and 2\% errors would give @@pd_SO_1p0_mlp@@\%, and the damping time adds nothing.
Probabilities are well calibrated, and conformal prediction sets built from one star per calibration EoS have empirical marginal coverage of 0.91--0.92 at the 0.90 target for stars of new EoS drawn from the same catalogue; this is an empirical result, not a distribution-free guarantee, because the folds are stratified and class-balanced.
Repeating the analysis on an alternative population (different parametrisations and priors) again shows no demonstrated nucleonic--dark-matter discrimination (@@Bpair_ALL_0@@\%) and a similar NICER-like accuracy (@@B_NICERpf20_mlp@@\%), whereas the @@nf_MLP-ens_ALL@@\% plateau and the identifiability of the hyperonic-like family depend on how that family is built.
This is a controlled identifiability and forecasting study: all results are conditional on the phenomenological EoS families, priors and error models defined here, and no inference about real stars is claimed.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Neutron stars probe matter at densities well above nuclear saturation \citep{LP04,OF16}, and a wide range of compositions---purely nucleonic matter, hyperon-rich matter, deconfined quark matter and neutron decay into a dark sector \citep{Husain22}---has been proposed.
Machine learning has been applied to neutron-star inference, for example to map observed masses and radii to the EoS \citep{Fujimoto18}. A natural question is how far a supervised classifier can distinguish composition from observables, and where the answer is limited by physics rather than by the algorithm.

A previous version of this work (arXiv:2512.23758v3) trained a small multilayer perceptron (MLP) on seven observables of @@nTot@@ synthetic stellar models, each class being one EoS, and reported a test accuracy of 97.4\% on 116 held-out models. That number does not support the conclusions drawn from it, and a single sequence per class cannot say how well compositions can be told apart when the EoS itself is uncertain. This revision therefore does the following.
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Audits the single-sequence benchmark} (Sec.~\ref{sec:legacy}): class sizes, mass coverage, a class-dependent redshift inconsistency, and the dependence of the accuracy on how test points are chosen.
\item \textbf{Builds a population of EoS} for each composition scenario and solves every one with a validated full-GR code (Sec.~\ref{sec:pop}); the checks (agreement with the six $f$-mode frequencies of an unpublished same-author cross-code pilot, analytic benchmarks and a published damping-time relation) are described in Sec.~\ref{sec:solver} and Table~\ref{tab:valid}.
\item \textbf{Evaluates with whole-EoS hold-out}, six learners and a kernel-Bayes reference classifier (Secs.~\ref{sec:methods}, \ref{sec:results}), under error scenarios taken from published measurements and forecasts as well as a scale that shows what precision would be needed.
\item \textbf{Quantifies uncertainty}: calibration, temperature scaling and conformal prediction sets with exchangeability at the level of whole EoS.
\end{enumerate}
Appendix~\ref{sec:legacynoise} keeps the single-sequence noise study, and Appendix~\ref{sec:changes} lists each claim of the earlier version that has been withdrawn or changed.

\section{What the single-sequence benchmark can and cannot show}\label{sec:legacy}
\subsection{Audit of the released data set}\label{sec:data}
The released data (\texttt{DataML.xlsx}) hold @@nTot@@ models of four classes: a nucleonic and a hyperonic quark--meson-coupling EoS \citep{Stone07}, a neutron-decay dark-matter scenario \citep{Husain22} and MIT-bag strange matter; the oscillation quantities come from the earlier oscillation code of \citet{HusainOsc} and are used as released here. Table~\ref{tab:audit} and Fig.~\ref{fig:audit} summarise the data.
