\subsection{Leakage from neighbouring models}\label{sec:leak}
Figure~\ref{fig:block} holds out contiguous blocks of increasing length (noise-free, 70/30 split, 15 repetitions). With all six observables ($M,R,f,Q,\tau,h_c$) the MLP accuracy decreases from @@blksixMLP1@@\% (single-model holdout, equivalent to a random split) to @@blksixMLP6@@\% at six-model blocks and @@blksixMLP24@@\% at 24; the random forest drops from @@blksixRF1@@\% to @@blksixRF6@@\% and @@blksixRF24@@\%. With the four plausible observables $\ftau$ the MLP values are @@blkfourMLP1@@\%, @@blkfourMLP6@@\% and @@blkfourMLP24@@\%. A large part of the apparent skill is therefore interpolation between near-duplicate neighbours of the same sequence. $Q$ and $h_c$ are model-derived amplitudes rather than measurable quantities and are not used further.
\begin{figure}[t]\centering\includegraphics[width=.95\textwidth]{figs/fig3_blocksize.pdf}
\caption{Noise-free test accuracy versus the length of the held-out contiguous mass block (mean and 95\% $t$-interval over 15 repetitions). Dotted: chance. A block length of one is equivalent to the random split of the earlier version.}\label{fig:block}\end{figure}
Even with the leakage removed, a single sequence per class cannot say whether the \emph{composition} is identifiable when the EoS is uncertain: ``classifying the family'' and ``recognising one EoS'' are the same task. The remainder of the paper addresses this with populations.

\section{A population of equations of state}\label{sec:pop}
\subsection{Composition scenarios as EoS populations}
Each scenario is a family of EoS defined by a small number of parameters with a uniform prior (Table~\ref{tab:priors}); one draw from the prior is one EoS. The families are phenomenological parametrisations \emph{inspired by} the four scenarios of the earlier version, not microphysical calculations.
\begin{table}[t]\centering\small
\caption{Parametrisations and priors. PP: piecewise polytrope \citep{Read09} with core break densities $\rho_1=10^{14.7}$ and $\rho_2=10^{15}$\,g\,cm$^{-3}$ on a four-piece crust; CSS: constant speed of sound, $\varepsilon=\varepsilon_0+p/c_s^2$.}\label{tab:priors}
\begin{tabular}{>{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{.17\textwidth}p{.75\textwidth}}\toprule
Scenario & Parameters (uniform priors)\\\midrule
Nucleonic & PP: $\log_{10}P_1\in[34.15,34.60]$ (dyn\,cm$^{-2}$ at $\rho_1$), $\Gamma_1\in[2.4,3.8]$, $\Gamma_2\in[2.4,3.9]$, $\Gamma_3\in[2.2,3.9]$\\
Hyperonic-like & PP with $\log_{10}P_1\in[34.20,34.60]$, $\Gamma_1\in[2.6,3.8]$ and a soft segment $\Gamma_2\in[1.8,2.6]$ above $\rho_1\approx2n_0$ (hyperon onset), then a causal CSS segment with $c_s^2\in[0.5,1]$ above $\rho_t=10^{u}$, $u\in[14.95,15.25]$\\
Strange (self-bound) & CSS with $\varepsilon_0=4B$, $B^{1/4}\in[130,160]$\,MeV, $c_s^2\in[1/3,1]$ ($c_s^2=1/3$ is the MIT bag model)\\
Dark-matter admixed & nucleonic PP prior plus a self-interacting dark Fermi gas in chemical equilibrium with the baryons (neutron-decay scenario): $m_\chi\in[937.9,938.783]$\,MeV (stability window), repulsive self-interaction $G\in[50,500]$\,fm$^2$; effective one-fluid barotrope\\\bottomrule\end{tabular}\end{table}
The dark-fermion mass window follows the neutron-decay dark-matter scenario \citep{FG18}: $m_\chi>937.9$\,MeV is required for the stability of $^{9}$Be and $m_\chi<m_p+m_e=938.783$\,MeV for the stability of $\chi$. Both bounds are imposed in the primary population, so every dark-matter EoS has $m_\chi$ inside the window. An earlier version of this analysis drew $m_\chi$ up to 939.2\,MeV, which exceeds the second bound for @@wide_nOut@@ of its 90 dark-matter EoS; that population is kept as a sensitivity run (Sec.~\ref{sec:robust}).
Draws are accepted only if they satisfy broad observational and consistency screens: $M_{\max}\ge1.97\,\Msun$, a causal speed of sound ($c_s^2\le1$) up to the centre of the maximum-mass star, $70\le\Lambda_{1.4}\le800$ and $10\le R_{1.4}\le14$\,km. The acceptance rates are @@acc0@@\%, @@acc1@@\%, @@acc2@@\% and @@acc3@@\% for the nucleonic, strange, dark-matter and hyperonic-like priors, so the screens remove most of the prior volume (mainly through $M_{\max}$ and causality, and for strange matter through $\Lambda_{1.4}$ and $R_{1.4}$). Observables are tabulated for stars of $1.2$--$1.9\,\Msun$ and evaluated on a mass grid over $1.2$--$1.85\,\Msun$, where all accepted EoS have stable stars.

The dark-matter-admixed scenario follows the effective-barotrope construction of the earlier work: baryons (nucleonic PP, $\mu_N=(\varepsilon+p)/n_B$) and a dark Fermi gas with $\mu_\chi=\sqrt{m_\chi^2+k_F^2}+\hbar cGn_\chi$ share one chemical potential, and the total pressure and energy density are summed at that potential. Because the dark component appears only above $\mu_N=m_\chi$, the stellar surface is purely baryonic and the dark component is at most a few per cent of the energy density even at the highest densities. By construction this scenario shares the nuclear prior with the nucleonic one, so the question the classifier faces is whether the dark component can be recognised against the spread of nuclear EoS.

\subsection{Solvers and validation}\label{sec:solver}
\paragraph{TOV and tidal deformability.} Stars are integrated with the Tolman--Oppenheimer--Volkoff equations \citep{Tolman39,OV39} and the tidal-deformability equation \citep{Hinderer08}.
\paragraph{Full-GR $f$-mode.} The $l=2$ quasi-normal $f$-mode of the non-rotating, barotropic star is computed from the linearised Einstein equations in the Regge--Wheeler gauge. The interior equations were derived symbolically (not taken from the literature) from the linearised Einstein equations and the conservation law, reduced to four first-order equations, and solved with regular boundary conditions at the centre and a vanishing Lagrangian pressure perturbation at the surface; this is the same physical system as the Lindblom--Detweiler formulation \citep{DL85}. The exterior solution is matched to the Zerilli equation \citep{Zerilli70}, and the complex eigenfrequency is the zero of the ingoing-wave amplitude; the real part gives $f$ and the imaginary part $\tau$. The exterior equations were checked against an independent symbolic derivation (residual $10^{-12}$). The complex eigenfrequency is found by a secant search on the analytic ingoing amplitude, starting within 3\% of the root.
\paragraph{Numerical error budget.} Varying one numerical setting at a time (interior and exterior integration tolerances from $10^{-8}$ to $10^{-13}$, the TOV tolerance, the surface cut-off, the starting radius at the centre and the outer matching radius from 1500 to 8000\,km) changes $f$ by at most @@convFmax@@ in relative terms and $\tau$ by at most @@convTmax@@, for SLy and for SLy with $G=100$\,fm$^2$ at $1.4\,\Msun$. These are about seven orders of magnitude smaller than the dark-matter shift of interest (median @@pairDfMed@@\%). The remaining sensitivity is physical: changing the crust pressure scale by $\pm10\%$ shifts $f$ of the nuclear EoS by @@crustDf@@\% but changes the dark-matter shift only from @@crustShiftLo@@\% to @@crustShiftHi@@\% (SLy, $G=100$\,fm$^2$), so the \emph{shift} is robust to the crust treatment even though absolute frequencies are not.
\paragraph{Damping times.} Damping times are compared with a published relation in the paragraph on published damping times below. The time dependence is $e^{i\omega t}$, so $\tau=1/\mathrm{Im}\,\omega$ and a damped mode has $\mathrm{Im}\,\omega>0$. The exterior machinery that produces $\mathrm{Im}\,\omega$ (Zerilli integration, projection onto asymptotic ingoing and outgoing solutions, complex root search) was benchmarked on a case with a published answer: with the horizon matched to purely ingoing waves it gives the fundamental $l=2$ quasi-normal mode of a Schwarzschild black hole, $M\omega=@@bhRe@@+@@bhIm@@\,i$, against $0.37367+0.08896\,i$ \citep{Leaver85}, i.e.\ relative errors of @@bhErrRe@@ (real part) and @@bhErrIm@@ (imaginary part). The interior contribution to $\tau$ at stellar compactness is tested only against a published fitting relation (below), with an offset of several per cent that is not understood, so statements about $\tau$ (in particular that it adds no classification information) are conditional on the solver.
\paragraph{Adiabatic response of the dark-matter configurations.} The perturbation equations use the equilibrium barotrope, i.e.\ chemical equilibrium is assumed to be maintained during the oscillation. As a bound on the opposite limit, $f$ was recomputed with a frozen-composition adiabatic response (baryon and dark numbers separately conserved, both components compressed together) for @@frN@@ dark-matter EoS at $1.4\,\Msun$: the median dark-matter shift is @@frShiftEq@@\% with the equilibrium response and @@frShiftFr@@\% with the frozen one, a difference of @@frRatio@@\% of the shift (at most @@frDMax@@\% of $f$). The adiabatic response of the dark sector therefore does not matter at the level of the effect. This does not test independent displacements of the two fluids, nor the equilibrium assumption for the nuclear composition itself, which affects the absolute frequencies of all classes alike.
\paragraph{Validation.} The crust is a single calibrated parameter (the absolute pressure scale of a four-piece SLy-like crust, @@valPscale@@ times the electron-gas value), fitted only to the radius and tidal deformability of six published EoS fits at $1.4\,\Msun$ (Table~\ref{tab:valid}); the radii are then reproduced to @@valRmax@@\,km and $\Lambda$ to @@valLmax@@\%. The $f$-mode frequencies were \emph{not} used in any fit; they agree with the pilot's to @@valFmax@@\% (maximum over the six EoS; ours are uniformly slightly higher). The reference values are those of the finite-EoS pilot of the earlier work (\citealt{HusainPilot}), which used the separately developed RelModPy full-GR code (\url{https://github.com/fgittins/RelModPy}); the six values are listed in Table~\ref{tab:valid} so that the comparison can be repeated with any other code. The pilot is an unpublished working manuscript by the same author (its numbers were computed with the third-party RelModPy code, but they have not been independently checked or published) and shares with the present work the Read et al.\ polytropic fits, the six reference EoS and the effective-barotrope construction of the dark-matter scenario; only the $f$-mode solver is a different implementation. This agreement is therefore a code-to-code consistency check and is \emph{not} an absolute accuracy of 0.007\% for stellar $f$-modes: the reference values come from the unpublished same-author pilot, and its inputs (EoS, crust, barotropic perturbation model) are shared, so errors common to both codes would not be detected. What the comparison shows is that two implementations solve the same linearised problem for the same barotropes. The independent tests of the solver are the analytic benchmarks and the comparison with a published relation below; no comparison with published numerical tables of $f$-modes for realistic EoS has been made. In addition, the dark-matter frequency shift at fixed nuclear EoS ($\mathrm{SLy}$, $G=100$\,fm$^2$, $1.4\,\Msun$) is @@valDM100@@\,Hz against @@valDM100ref@@\,Hz in the reference, and a tabulated version of the same EoS agrees with the analytic one to @@valTab@@\% in $f$. Damping times of $@@valTauMin@@$--$@@valTauMax@@$\,s are obtained for the six EoS, as expected for a $1.4\,\Msun$ $f$-mode; the pilot reference values do not include $\tau$, so $\tau$ is compared with a published relation below.
\paragraph{Analytic benchmarks with a finite surface density.} Three exact Newtonian results for the homogeneous incompressible star test the solver with a density jump at the surface, as in self-bound strange matter: the Kelvin $f$-mode, $\omega^2=2l(l-1)/(2l+1)\,GM/R^3=0.8\,GM/R^3$ for $l=2$, the tidal Love number $k_2=3/4$, and the quadrupole-formula damping time of the Kelvin mode, $\tau_N=19.53125\,R^4/M^3$ ($G=c=1$). The GR solver was run on a nearly incompressible constant-density EoS ($c_s^2=10^4$) at compactnesses $C=M/R$ from 0.002 to 0.04; the deviations from the Newtonian values vanish linearly with $C$ as they must (at $C=@@anaC@@$ the frequency exceeds the Kelvin value by @@anaDf0@@, and the linear extrapolation to $C\to0$ gives @@anaDfInt@@). For the Love number, the matching at the surface requires the correction for the discontinuity in the density \citep{Postnikov10,Damour09}: with it $k_2=@@anaK@@$ at $C=@@anaC@@$ and the extrapolation to $C\to0$ is within @@anaKint@@\% of $3/4$ (slope @@anaKslope@@ per unit $C$), whereas without it $k_2=@@anaKunc@@$. The damping time approaches $\tau_N$ in the same way (the deviation extrapolates to @@anaTauInt@@ at $C\to0$, slope @@anaTauSlope@@ per unit $C$), which tests the interior--exterior coupling that produces $\mathrm{Im}\,\omega$ in the weak-field limit. This tests the $f$-mode, damping-time and tidal matching at a finite surface density, but only in the Newtonian limit; the numerical convergence for self-bound stars (the MIT bag and a pQCD-form EoS) gives changes of at most @@cvSf@@ in $f$ and @@cvSt@@ in $\tau$ for the same variations of the numerical settings as above.
\paragraph{Comparison with a published full-GR relation for $\tau$.} \citet{LS18} fitted the full-GR damping times of nine nuclear EoS for $M/R>0.1$ with $M/\tau=0.112x^4-0.53x^5+0.628x^6$ ($x=M/R$, $G=c=1$; their Eq.~20, quoted correlation coefficient 0.9997). At $1.4\,\Msun$ the damping times of the six reference EoS (compactness @@pubSixCmin@@--@@pubSixCmax@@) lie \textbf{@@pubSixMin@@ to @@pubSixMax@@\% above} this relation, and the relation of Andersson and Kokkotas (their Eq.~18 as quoted by \citealt{LS18}) is exceeded by @@pubAKMin@@--@@pubAKMax@@\%. In the nuclear populations the median offsets are @@pubA_nuc@@\% (nucleonic), @@pubA_dm@@\% (dark-matter) and @@pubA_hyp@@\% (hyperonic-like) for population~A and @@pubB_nuc@@ to @@pubB_hyp@@\% for population~B; self-bound stars lie far outside the relation (@@pubA_str@@\% and @@pubB_str@@\%), which is expected because it was fitted to nuclear EoS without a surface density jump. The origin of the 3--7\% offset for the reference EoS has not been identified; the Newtonian-limit benchmark above and the exterior black-hole benchmark rule out a gross error in the damping-time machinery but not a few-per-cent error at stellar compactness (possible causes include the crust model, EoS differences from the fitting sample and the accuracy of the fit itself, none of which was tested). The absolute damping times should therefore be regarded as uncertain at the level of several per cent; because $\tau$ adds no classification information in any experiment, no conclusion of this paper depends on it.
