\section{Methods}\label{sec:methods}
\subsection{Whole-EoS validation}
Every EoS belongs to exactly one of five folds (stratified by scenario). In each of five rounds one fold is the test set, the next fold is the calibration set and the other three are the training set; no EoS appears in more than one role, so every reported number is for EoS the classifier has never seen. The partition is repeated for three random seeds; we report the mean over folds within a repetition and Student-$t$ 95\% intervals over the three repetitions. Observations are $(M,R,f,\tau)$ at masses drawn uniformly over $1.2$--$1.85\,\Msun$ (a mass prior shared by all classes), with independent noise draws, and classes are balanced (chance 25\%).

\subsection{Observational-error scenarios}\label{sec:errors}
Observables are perturbed multiplicatively, $x_j^{\rm obs}=x_j(1+\sigma_j\epsilon_j)$, $\epsilon_j\sim\mathcal N(0,1)$ independent. Draws with a non-positive factor (only possible for the damping time at large $s$: 0.6\% of the draws at $s=2$ and 11\% at $s=4$; none elsewhere) are redrawn, so the noise is a truncated Gaussian; the kernel-Bayes likelihood ignores this truncation.
\emph{Literature-anchored scenarios:}
\textbf{NICER-like}: $\sigma_M=10\%$, $\sigma_R=9\%$, no $f$ or $\tau$; these are the 68\% uncertainties of the NICER analyses of PSR J0030+0451 ($M=1.34^{+0.15}_{-0.16}$ and $1.44^{+0.15}_{-0.14}\,\Msun$, $R=12.71^{+1.14}_{-1.19}$ and $13.02^{+1.24}_{-1.06}$\,km; \citealt{Riley19,Miller19}).
\textbf{NICER-like plus $f$-mode}: in addition $\sigma_f=20\%$, motivated by the forecast that third-generation detectors could measure the $f$-mode frequency of a GW170817-like inspiral to within a few hundred Hz \citep{Williams22}, i.e.\ roughly 10--25\% of $f$. These two are illustrative combinations drawn from different sources (a NICER pulsar and a hypothetical GW170817-like inspiral), not a realistic joint observation: mass, radius and $f$-mode of a single object are not currently available together.
\emph{Precision scale:} to show what would be required, the same observables are also evaluated with errors $s\times(5\%,5\%,2\%,20\%)$ in $(M,R,f,\tau)$ for $s\in\{0.25,0.5,1,2,4\}$, and in the noise-free limit. These values are not forecasts for any instrument; they define a scale on which the literature-anchored scenarios can be placed. Three observable sets are used: $\{M,R\}$, $\{M,R,f\}$ and $\{M,R,f,\tau\}$.

\subsection{Classifiers}
Learners: logistic regression, $k$-nearest neighbours ($k=25$), a random forest (200 trees, minimum leaf 5), histogram gradient boosting, an RBF support-vector machine (8000 training samples) and an MLP deep ensemble \citep{Lakshminarayanan17} of three $2\times64$ ReLU networks (the architecture of the earlier version) on log-transformed, standardised inputs. Training data contain three noise realisations of 33 masses for each training EoS.

\subsection{Kernel-Bayes reference classifier}
For a reference set of $(\mathrm{EoS},M)$ points the posterior of class $k$ for an observation $\mathbf x^{\rm obs}$ is
\begin{equation}
P(k\mid\mathbf x^{\rm obs})\;\propto\;\frac1{N_k}\sum_{i\in k}\prod_j\mathcal N\!\left(x^{\rm obs}_j;\,x_{ij},\,(\sigma_jx_{ij})^2\right),
\end{equation}
which is the Bayes-optimal classifier for the stated error model and a uniform prior over the reference EoS and masses. We use the training EoS as reference (the same information as the learners) and, separately, all EoS except the test fold (a larger reference). Unlike the single-sequence oracle of Appendix~\ref{sec:legacynoise}, this is not an upper bound, because the test EoS are not in the reference set; agreement between learners and kernel-Bayes indicates that the remaining error is set by the information in the observables rather than by the learner.

\subsection{Uncertainty quantification}\label{sec:uncmethods}
\emph{Calibration}: expected calibration error (ECE, 15 bins), negative log-likelihood (NLL) and Brier score, before and after temperature scaling fitted on the calibration EoS. \emph{Epistemic uncertainty}: mutual information across ensemble members. \emph{Conformal prediction}: split conformal prediction with the score $1-\hat p(y\mid x)$ at target miscoverage $\alpha=0.1$ \citep{Vovk05,Angelopoulos23}, calibrated on the calibration EoS with \emph{one star per calibration EoS}: for each of the $n\approx72$ calibration EoS one star (random mass, one noise draw) is chosen, so that the calibration units are independent (EoS, star) pairs that are exchangeable with a test star drawn the same way from a new EoS of the same population. The score quantile is taken at level $\lceil(n+1)(1-\alpha)\rceil/n$, as in standard split conformal prediction, and the reported sets and coverage are averages over 40 random choices of the calibration stars. The textbook finite-sample guarantee requires the calibration and test units to be exchangeable. Here the folds are stratified and class-balanced: the calibration fold contains a fixed number of EoS per class, the test EoS come from a disjoint fold with the same composition, and both are drawn without replacement from a finite catalogue. Exchangeability then holds within each class and for the class-balanced mixture only approximately (conditioning on the fixed class counts of the calibration fold breaks it by an amount of order $1/n$), so we do not claim the exact distribution-free guarantee; the coverage reported is an \emph{empirical marginal coverage} for stars of unseen EoS drawn from the same catalogue distribution with a uniform class prior. As a sensitivity check, a pooled variant that uses all stars of the calibration EoS (33 masses, two noise draws each) with the same $n$ is also reported; its stars are correlated, so for it even the approximate exchangeability argument fails. The coverage is marginal, not conditional on class or mass, and assumes that the EoS of the catalogue are exchangeable draws from one population; it does not extend to EoS from another population (Sec.~\ref{sec:popB}).

\section{Results}\label{sec:results}
\subsection{Noise-free separability of EoS populations}
Figure~\ref{fig:nf} shows the held-out-EoS accuracy without measurement error, and the left panel of Fig.~\ref{fig:cmpop} the confusion matrix. With $M$ and $R$ only the accuracy is @@nf_MLP-ens_S@@\% (MLP ensemble; logistic regression @@nf_LogReg_S@@\%, random forest @@nf_RandForest_S@@\%); adding the $f$-mode raises it to @@nf_MLP-ens_SF@@\% and adding $\tau$ changes nothing (@@nf_MLP-ens_SO@@\%). All learners agree within a few points. The confusion matrix shows why the ceiling is so low: strange matter is recognised perfectly (@@cm_ALL_0_11@@\%), hyperonic-like matter in @@cm_ALL_0_33@@\% of cases, but nucleonic and dark-matter-admixed stars are assigned to each other almost at random (recall @@cm_ALL_0_00@@\% and @@cm_ALL_0_22@@\%; accuracy between the two classes @@pairNucDM_ALL_0@@\%, chance 50\%). Merging them into one class gives @@merged_ALL_0@@\% three-class accuracy; the baseline for this merged problem is 50\% (always predicting the merged class, which holds half of the stars), not the 33\% of three balanced classes. The reason is physical: the dark component shifts $f$ by only @@pairDfMed@@\% at fixed nuclear EoS (median over the population; @@valDM100@@\,Hz or 1.3\% for SLy with $G=100$\,fm$^2$; Sec.~\ref{sec:paired}), far less than the spread of $f$ at fixed mass produced by the uncertain nuclear EoS (about $\pm10\%$; Table~\ref{tab:popdata}).
\begin{figure}[t]\centering\includegraphics[width=.75\textwidth]{figs/figP2_noisefree.pdf}
\caption{Held-out-EoS accuracy without measurement error for each learner and observable set (95\% $t$-intervals over three repetitions; dotted: chance).}\label{fig:nf}\end{figure}

\subsection{Errors anchored on published measurements}\label{sec:anchored}
Table~\ref{tab:scen} gives the accuracy under the literature-anchored scenarios and at selected points of the precision scale. With NICER-like errors ($M$ 10\%, $R$ 9\%) the accuracy is @@sc_NICER_mlp@@\% (MLP ensemble; kernel-Bayes @@sc_NICER_bk@@\%), barely above the 25\% chance level; adding a 20\% $f$-mode measurement raises it to @@sc_NICERpf20_mlp@@\%. The three-class accuracy after merging nucleonic and dark-matter-admixed matter is @@merged_NICERpf20@@\%, no better than the 50\% majority-class baseline of the merged problem. Errors of 5\%, 5\% and 2\% in $(M,R,f)$ would give @@pd_SO_1p0_mlp@@\%, and 1.3\%, 1.3\%, 0.5\% would give @@pd_SO_0p25_mlp@@\%, which approaches but never exceeds the noise-free ceiling of @@nf_MLP-ens_ALL@@\%.
