\subsection{Precision scale and which observable matters}
Figure~\ref{fig:dial} shows the accuracy against the precision scale. For the MLP ensemble, $\{M,R\}$ gives @@pd_S_0p25_mlp@@\%, @@pd_S_1p0_mlp@@\% and @@pd_S_4p0_mlp@@\% at $s=0.25$, 1 and 4, whereas $\{M,R,f\}$ gives @@pd_SF_0p25_mlp@@\%, @@pd_SF_1p0_mlp@@\% and @@pd_SF_4p0_mlp@@\%; the damping time with a 20\% error (scaled with $s$) adds nothing within the uncertainty. Learners and kernel-Bayes agree to a few points at every scale (at $s=1$: MLP @@pd_SO_1p0_mlp@@\%, kernel-Bayes @@pd_SO_1p0_bk@@\%, kernel-Bayes with all other EoS as reference @@pd_SO_1p0_bk2@@\%; logistic regression @@pd_SO_1p0_lr@@\%, random forest @@pd_SO_1p0_rf@@\%, gradient boosting @@pd_SO_1p0_hgb@@\%, SVM @@pd_SO_1p0_svm@@\%), so none of the learners tried leaves a measurable amount of information unused, and enlarging the reference sample from the training EoS to all other EoS does not change the result (this is evidence for an information plateau within these priors, not a proof of an upper bound).
\begin{figure}[t]\centering\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figs/figP3_dial.pdf}
\caption{Held-out-EoS accuracy versus precision scale $s$ (1 = 5\%, 5\%, 2\%, 20\% in $M,R,f,\tau$) for the MLP ensemble and the kernel-Bayes classifier, for three observable sets (95\% $t$-intervals; dotted: chance). The $\{M,R,f\}$ and $\{M,R,f,\tau\}$ curves coincide.}\label{fig:dial}\end{figure}

\subsection{Degeneracies}
Figure~\ref{fig:cmpop} shows row-normalised confusion matrices. With accurate $f$ ($s=0.25$) strange matter is still identified perfectly (@@cm_SO_0p25_11@@\%) and the hyperonic-like class in @@cm_SO_0p25_33@@\%, but the nucleonic and dark-matter rows remain mixed (@@cm_SO_0p25_00@@\% and @@cm_SO_0p25_22@@\% correct, @@cm_SO_0p25_02@@\% and @@cm_SO_0p25_20@@\% assigned to each other). With a 20\% $f$-mode error all rows are confused with all columns, strange matter being the best recovered (@@cm_NICERpf20_11@@\%).
\begin{figure}[t]\centering\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figs/figP4_confusion.pdf}
\caption{Row-normalised confusion matrices (\%) of the temperature-scaled MLP ensemble, pooled over folds and repetitions. Left to right: noise-free, $s=0.25$, and NICER-like errors plus a 20\% $f$-mode measurement.}\label{fig:cmpop}\end{figure}

\subsection{Dark matter when the nuclear EoS is known}\label{sec:paired}
To separate ``the dark component is small'' from ``the nuclear EoS is unknown'', the observables of each of the @@pairN@@ dark-matter-admixed EoS were also computed for its own nuclear base EoS without the dark component, at the same masses. At fixed nuclear EoS and mass the dark component raises $f$ by a median of @@pairDfMed@@\% (EoS-level bootstrap interval of the median @@pairDfMed_lo@@--@@pairDfMed_hi@@\%, conditional on the construction below; 5--95\% range @@pairDfP5@@--@@pairDfP95@@\%; positive for @@pairDfPos@@\% of the pairs) and lowers $R$ by a median of @@pairDRMed@@\% (@@pairDRP5@@ to @@pairDRP95@@\%); the shift falls steeply with the self-interaction (Fig.~\ref{fig:paired}a; correlation of $\ln G$ with $\Delta f/f$ at $1.4\,\Msun$ is @@pairCorrG@@). \emph{The shift is not independent of the model construction:} when the baryon-number normalisation of the one-fluid barotrope is changed to its extreme value, the shift of an individual EoS changes by a median of @@presRelMed@@\% and at most @@presRelMax@@\% (and by up to @@presWideOutMax@@\% for EoS with $m_\chi$ above the stability window in the wide-prior sample; see the paragraph on the dark-matter construction below), and the bootstrap interval reflects the EoS sample, not this modelling uncertainty. If the observer were told the nuclear EoS exactly, the Bayes accuracy for dark matter versus no dark matter (equal priors, independent Gaussian errors, mass identical by construction) would reach 75\% at $\sigma_f=@@pairSf_10_75@@\%$ (EoS-level bootstrap interval @@pairSf_10_75_lo@@--@@pairSf_10_75_hi@@\%) when $R$ is known to 1\% (@@pairSf_50_75@@\% for 5\%) and 90\% at $\sigma_f=@@pairSf_50_90@@\%$ (@@pairSf_50_90_lo@@--@@pairSf_50_90_hi@@\%) (Fig.~\ref{fig:paired}b); at the points of the precision scale it is @@pairAcc_s0p25@@\% at $s=0.25$ (0.5\% in $f$, 1.25\% in $R$), @@pairAcc_s1p0@@\% at $s=1$ and @@pairAcc_s4p0@@\% at $s=4$, and with a 20\% $f$-mode error and 9\% in $R$ it is @@pairAccf_200@@\%. With the nuclear EoS \emph{unknown} the accuracy between the nucleonic and dark-matter classes is @@pairNucDM_ALL_0@@\% even without any measurement error (Sec.~\ref{sec:results}). The lack of discrimination therefore comes from the uncertainty of the nuclear EoS, not from the smallness of the shift alone: detecting the dark component needs both an $f$-mode measurement at the 0.5\% level and a nuclear EoS known to better than the shift itself.
\paragraph{Sensitivity to the dark-matter construction.} The one-fluid barotrope assigns baryon number through $n_B=\rho/m_0$. This paper uses $m_0=931.494$\,MeV (the atomic mass unit, the normalisation of the polytropic fits); the companion pilot \citep{HusainPilot} uses $m_0=931.192$\,MeV. The baryon chemical potential $\mu_N\to m_0$ at low density, so $m_0$ fixes where $\mu_N$ exceeds $m_\chi$ and the dark component appears, and it is not a free parameter of the dark-matter physics but a convention of the effective barotrope. The two values give the same shift to within @@presRelMed_d@@\% for a typical EoS (at most @@presRelMax_d@@\% over the @@presN@@ EoS tested; for SLy with $G=100$\,fm$^2$, @@valDM100@@\,Hz here against @@valDM100ref@@\,Hz in the pilot, a difference that also includes the different solvers), so the reconciliation of the two normalisations does not matter at the level of the headline number. Larger changes of convention move the population median very little: with $m_0=935$ and $938.9$\,MeV (the latter places the threshold at zero density) the median shifts at $1.4\,\Msun$ are @@presMed_b@@\% and @@presMed_c@@\% against @@presMed_a@@\% (5--95\% ranges @@presLo_b@@--@@presHi_b@@ and @@presLo_c@@--@@presHi_c@@\% against @@presLo_a@@--@@presHi_a@@\%), but for an individual EoS of the primary (in-window) population the shift changes by a median of @@presRelMed@@\% and at most @@presRelMax@@\% between the default and the extreme prescription. The sensitivity is much larger when $m_\chi$ lies above the stability window: for the @@presWideOutN@@ EoS of the earlier wide-prior sample with $m_\chi>938.783$\,MeV (of @@presWideN@@ tested) the change has a median of @@presWideOutMed@@\% and a maximum of @@presWideOutMax@@\%, the largest changes (23--65\%) occurring for three EoS with $m_\chi=939.00$--939.03\,MeV, just above the extreme normalisation of 938.9\,MeV; the in-window EoS of that sample change by at most @@presWideInMax@@\%. The shift depends much more on $G$ (correlation of $\ln G$ with $\Delta f/f$: @@pairCorrG@@) than on $m_\chi$ (correlation @@pairCorrM@@). The qualitative conclusion is unchanged, but the shift of a single EoS depends on the prescription at the per-cent level inside the window (and strongly outside it), so the quantitative detectability thresholds above (e.g.\ the $f$-mode precision for 75\% accuracy) carry this modelling uncertainty in addition to their statistical intervals, and the effective one-fluid oscillation model (equilibrium composition, common displacement of the two components) remains an explicit assumption of every dark-matter statement in this paper.
\begin{figure}[t]\centering\includegraphics[width=.95\textwidth]{figs/figP6_paired.pdf}
\caption{Dark matter at fixed nuclear EoS (@@pairN@@ paired EoS). (a) Fractional $f$-mode shift at $1.4\,\Msun$ versus the self-interaction $G$. (b) Bayes accuracy for dark matter versus no dark matter, averaged over EoS and masses, as a function of the $f$-mode error for three radius errors (dotted: chance).}\label{fig:paired}\end{figure}

\subsection{Calibration and prediction sets}
The probabilities are well calibrated because the learners are close to the information limit (Fig.~\ref{fig:uncpop}a): at $s=1$ the ECE is @@pu_1p0_r_ece@@ for the raw ensemble, @@pu_1p0_t_ece@@ after temperature scaling and @@pu_1p0_b_ece@@ for kernel-Bayes, with NLL @@pu_1p0_t_nll@@ nats for all three (Table~\ref{tab:uncpop}). Conformal sets calibrated with one star per EoS have empirical marginal coverage of 0.91--0.92 at the 0.90 target for every scenario (as expected for the finite-sample-corrected quantile; no formal guarantee is claimed for the stratified folds, Sec.~\ref{sec:uncmethods}); the pooled variant gives 0.91--0.92 with almost identical sets (@@puP_1p0_t_setsize@@ against @@pu_1p0_t_setsize@@ at $s=1$). The price of validity is set size: @@pu_NICER_t_setsize@@ of four classes under NICER-like errors, @@pu_NICERpf20_t_setsize@@ when a 20\% $f$-mode is added, @@pu_1p0_t_setsize@@ at $s=1$ and @@pu_0p25_t_setsize@@ at $s=0.25$. Temperature scaling and kernel-Bayes give the same sets. At realistic precision the data therefore support a short list containing most of the classes, not a single label.
