\subsection{Statistical robustness}\label{sec:robust}
\paragraph{EoS-level bootstrap.} The intervals of the preceding sections describe re-partitioning of the same 360 EoS. Table~\ref{tab:boot} instead gives 95\% intervals from resampling whole EoS within each class (2000 resamples; each EoS's predictions pooled over the three partitions). These intervals are \emph{conditional} on the catalogue and the trained classifiers: they quantify which EoS happen to be in the test sets, but not the variability from regenerating the EoS populations from the priors, from retraining the classifiers on a different catalogue, or from changing the priors themselves (the last two are only partly probed by the learning curves and by population~B). The noise-free accuracy of the MLP ensemble is @@bs_ALL_0_MLP-ens_acc@@\% [@@bs_ALL_0_MLP-ens_acc_lo@@, @@bs_ALL_0_MLP-ens_acc_hi@@], the recall of strange matter is 100\% [100, 100], and the accuracy between the nucleonic and dark-matter classes is @@bs_ALL_0_MLP-ens_pair@@\% [@@bs_ALL_0_MLP-ens_pair_lo@@, @@bs_ALL_0_MLP-ens_pair_hi@@]; with NICER-like errors and a 20\% $f$-mode it is @@bs_NICERpf20_MLP-ens_pair@@\% [@@bs_NICERpf20_MLP-ens_pair_lo@@, @@bs_NICERpf20_MLP-ens_pair_hi@@] and the overall accuracy @@bs_NICERpf20_MLP-ens_acc@@\% [@@bs_NICERpf20_MLP-ens_acc_lo@@, @@bs_NICERpf20_MLP-ens_acc_hi@@]. The intervals for the nucleonic--dark-matter pair contain 50\% in every scenario, so the absence of demonstrated discrimination is not an artefact of a particular partition of this finite sample; it remains conditional on the catalogue and priors, and an interval containing 50\% does not prove that the classes are identical, only that these experiments do not show a difference.
