\paragraph{Wider dark-fermion mass prior (sensitivity).} The primary population restricts $m_\chi$ to the stability window $937.9\le m_\chi\le938.783$\,MeV. As a sensitivity experiment the earlier population, whose dark-matter EoS drew $m_\chi$ up to 939.2\,MeV (@@wide_nOut@@ of its 90 dark-matter EoS above the upper bound and @@wide_nIn@@ inside), was analysed with the same protocol; the other three classes are identical. The results are essentially the same as for the primary population: @@wide_ALL_0_mlp@@\% without noise, @@wide_SO_0p25_mlp@@\% at $s=0.25$, @@wide_SO_1p0_mlp@@\% at $s=1$ and @@wide_NICERpf20_mlp@@\% for NICER-like errors plus a 20\% $f$-mode (MLP ensemble), with an accuracy of @@wide_pair@@\% between the nucleonic and dark-matter classes (primary: @@nf_MLP-ens_ALL@@\%, @@pd_SO_0p25_mlp@@\%, @@pd_SO_1p0_mlp@@\%, @@sc_NICERpf20_mlp@@\% and @@pairNucDM_ALL_0@@\%).

\subsection{Robustness to the EoS populations}\label{sec:popB}
The preceding results depend on the parametrisations and priors of Table~\ref{tab:priors}. To test this, an alternative population (``B'') was constructed with different functional forms and priors (Table~\ref{tab:priorsB}); the same screens, solver and experiments were applied (48 EoS per scenario, 192 in total; Table~\ref{tab:popBdata}). Population~B is still parametric rather than microphysical, so it tests sensitivity to the parametrisation, not to the underlying nuclear physics.
