Scenario & $N$ & $M_{\max}$ & $R_{1.4}$ & $\Lambda_{1.4}$ & $f_{1.4}$\\\midrule\input{tables/tab_popB_data}\\\bottomrule\end{tabular}\end{table}
\paragraph{Within population B.} With whole-EoS hold-out, the MLP ensemble reaches @@B_ALL_0_mlp@@\% without noise (logistic regression @@B_ALL_0_lr@@\%, random forest @@B_ALL_0_rf@@\%), against @@nf_MLP-ens_ALL@@\% for population~A. Strange matter is again recognised perfectly (@@Bcm_ALL_0_1@@\%) and the accuracy between the nucleonic and dark-matter classes is again at the level of chance (@@Bpair_ALL_0@@\%); nucleonic matter is recognised in @@Bcm_ALL_0_0@@\%, dark-matter in @@Bcm_ALL_0_2@@\% and hyperonic-like matter in only @@Bcm_ALL_0_3@@\% (@@cm_ALL_0_33@@\% in population~A), so the separability of the hyperonic-like family, and with it the overall plateau, depends on how the family is built. At $s=0.25$ and $s=1$ the accuracies are @@B_SO_0p25_mlp@@\% and @@B_SO_1p0_mlp@@\% (A: @@pd_SO_0p25_mlp@@\% and @@pd_SO_1p0_mlp@@\%), and for NICER-like errors plus a 20\% $f$-mode @@B_NICERpf20_mlp@@\% (A: @@sc_NICERpf20_mlp@@\%); there strange matter is recognised in @@Bcm_NICERpf20_1@@\% of cases.
\paragraph{Out-of-family transfer.} Table~\ref{tab:cross} gives the accuracy of classifiers trained on all EoS of one population and tested on all EoS of the other. Without noise the transfer accuracy is @@X_AB_ALL_0_MLP-ens@@\% (A$\to$B) and @@X_BA_ALL_0_MLP-ens@@\% (B$\to$A) for the MLP ensemble, between the within-population values, and with NICER-like errors plus a 20\% $f$-mode @@X_AB_NICERpf20_MLP-ens@@\% and @@X_BA_NICERpf20_MLP-ens@@\%. Strange matter is still recognised in @@Xcm_AB_ALL_0_1@@\% and @@Xcm_BA_ALL_0_1@@\% of cases without noise, and the accuracy between the nucleonic and dark-matter classes remains @@Xpair_AB_ALL_0@@\% and @@Xpair_BA_ALL_0@@\%. The results that survive a change of population are therefore (i) the absence of demonstrated nucleonic--dark-matter discrimination, (ii) an accuracy of about 33--37\% under NICER-like errors, with or without a 20\% $f$-mode, and (iii) the identifiability of self-bound strange matter once $f$ is accurate; the @@nf_MLP-ens_ALL@@\% plateau and the identifiability of the hyperonic-like family are specific to the construction of population~A.
\paragraph{Prior width.} To probe how the nucleonic--dark-matter accuracy depends on the width of the nuclear prior, the main population was restricted to EoS whose four polytropic parameters all lie in the central fraction $w$ of their prior ranges (the same restriction for both classes; Table~\ref{tab:pw}). For $w=1$, 0.9, 0.8 and 0.7 (down to 32 nucleonic and 23 dark-matter EoS) the accuracy of logistic regression and of a random forest stays between 45\% and 61\% in the noise-free case and at $s=0.25$ and $s=1$. The highest value (61\%, random forest, noise-free, $w=0.7$) rests on 55 EoS, for which the binomial standard error is about 7 points, and it is not accompanied by a rise in the other settings or for logistic regression, so the values are compatible with chance at these sample sizes; narrowing the prior by 30\% per parameter does not make the dark component recognisable in these experiments. Substantially narrower priors would leave too few EoS in this catalogue, and the limit of a known nuclear EoS ($w\to0$), where the dark component can be detected at the sub-per-cent level, is treated in Sec.~\ref{sec:paired}.
