\section{Discussion and limitations}
\paragraph{What the results support.} (i) A high accuracy on single-sequence data does not carry over to populations: with whole-EoS hold-out the highest accuracy reached by any method for these four scenarios is about @@nf_MLP-ens_ALL@@\% even without measurement error (@@B_ALL_0_mlp@@\% in the alternative population B). (ii) The plateau looks informational: six different learners (including the MLP ensemble) and a kernel-Bayes classifier built on the exact error model agree, so within these priors a better learner is unlikely to help (the kernel-Bayes classifier uses a finite reference population and is not a rigorous upper bound). (iii) With the errors of current X-ray measurements and a plausible third-generation $f$-mode measurement, composition is essentially not identified in these experiments (@@sc_NICER_mlp@@--@@sc_NICERpf20_mlp@@\% against 25\% chance); an accurate $f$-mode is the observable that would help, and the damping time adds nothing. (iv) In the populations tested, no discrimination between dark-matter-admixed and nucleonic matter is demonstrated unless the nuclear EoS is known to better than the shift in $f$ that the dark component produces (median @@pairDfMed@@\%, up to about 2\% for weak self-interaction); with the nuclear EoS known exactly, an $f$-mode precision of about @@pairSf_10_75@@\% would give 75\% accuracy (Sec.~\ref{sec:paired}), in line with the EoS-conditional analysis of the earlier pilot. (v) Probabilities and conformal sets are reliable and turn the lack of discrimination into an explicit statement about which classes remain plausible.

\paragraph{Limitations.}
\emph{Phenomenological families.} The EoS families are parametrisations with uniform priors, screened by $M_{\max}$, causality, $\Lambda_{1.4}$ and $R_{1.4}$; conclusions depend on these priors. The absence of demonstrated nucleonic--dark-matter discrimination in particular follows from the dark-matter scenario sharing the nuclear prior. Microphysical models (for example a population of QMC EoS) would change the priors and might make some classes easier or harder to separate. The hyperonic-like family is a soft-then-stiff parametrisation, not a hyperonic calculation, and the strange family uses a constant speed of sound; the classifier therefore separates parametrisation families. A second population with different functional forms and priors (Sec.~\ref{sec:popB}) again shows no demonstrated nucleonic--dark-matter discrimination, a similar NICER-like accuracy and the recognition of strange matter (self-bound models with a surface density jump give a distinct $f$--$R$ relation in both), but not the @@nf_MLP-ens_ALL@@\% plateau or the identifiability of the hyperonic-like family. No test with microphysically computed EoS (for example hyperonic or quark-matter calculations) has been made, and the conclusions about composition remain conditional on parametric families.
\emph{Dark matter.} The dark component is treated as an effective one-fluid barotrope in chemical equilibrium; the check against the two-fluid pilot is a single number ($@@valDM100@@$ versus $@@valDM100ref@@$\,Hz), a frozen-composition response changes the shift by only @@frRatio@@\% (Sec.~\ref{sec:solver}), but the assignment of baryon number to the fitted barotrope changes the shift of an individual EoS by up to @@presRelMax@@\% inside the stability window and by up to @@presWideOutMax@@\% for dark-fermion masses above it (Sec.~\ref{sec:paired}). Independent displacement of the two fluids (a genuine two-fluid eigenmode calculation), other dark-sector models and other dark fractions are not explored.
\emph{Solver.} Non-rotating stars, $l=2$ only, adiabatic perturbations equal to the equilibrium barotrope (no composition gradients, entrainment or crust elasticity); the damping time is checked only through the exterior black-hole benchmark, the Newtonian-limit benchmark and a published universal relation, from which it differs by @@pubSixMin@@--@@pubSixMax@@\% for the reference EoS (unexplained); the crust has one calibrated parameter; and no comparison with published numerical $f$-mode tables for realistic EoS has been made (the six reference values come from an unpublished same-author pilot).
\emph{Errors.} Independent multiplicative Gaussian errors are used; the NICER and $f$-mode values are anchored on published results and forecasts for individual sources, not on a population of detections, and systematic theory error and correlated errors are not modelled.
\emph{Statistics and sampling.} 90 EoS per scenario (48 in population B) and three fold partitions that reuse the same EoS, so the $t$-intervals reflect partition and noise variability only; EoS-level bootstrap intervals and learning curves are given in Sec.~\ref{sec:robust}. A uniform mass prior over $1.2$--$1.85\,\Msun$ is used; the conformal coverage is empirical and marginal only (no exact guarantee is claimed for the stratified folds), and the bootstrap intervals are conditional on the catalogue and classifiers (they do not include regenerating the EoS populations or retraining).
\emph{Observational realism.} The errors are independent and Gaussian; real NICER posteriors are correlated and non-Gaussian, and the GW170817 and NICER posteriors themselves were not used.
\emph{Not comparable to earlier data.} The new data come from a new solver and cannot be compared point by point with the single-sequence data set.

\section{Conclusions}
Evaluated on populations of EoS with whole-EoS hold-out, machine-learning classification of neutron-star composition is far less decisive than the 97\% single-sequence benchmark suggested: the accuracy plateau is about @@nf_MLP-ens_ALL@@\% even without measurement error (@@B_ALL_0_mlp@@\% in an alternative population with different parametrisations), it appears to be set by the information in the observables rather than by the learner, strange matter is the only scenario that is cleanly separable (in the family defined here), and no discrimination between nucleonic and dark-matter-admixed matter is demonstrated in these experiments when the nuclear EoS is unknown (the dark component shifts $f$ by a median of @@pairDfMed@@\% at fixed nuclear EoS, a shift that depends on the baryon-number prescription of the one-fluid model by a median of @@presRelMed@@\% (at most @@presRelMax@@\%) for an individual EoS inside the stability window and by up to @@presWideOutMax@@\% above it). With errors anchored on current X-ray measurements and a forecast $f$-mode precision the accuracy is @@sc_NICER_mlp@@--@@sc_NICERpf20_mlp@@\%, and calibrated prediction sets contain about @@pu_NICERpf20_t_setsize@@ of four classes.

\section*{Data, code and reproducibility}
The single-sequence analysis uses \texttt{audit.py}, \texttt{repro.py}, \texttt{blocksize.py}, \texttt{main.py}, \texttt{extrap.py}, \texttt{interp\_check.py} and \texttt{report.py}; the population study uses the solver and generator in \texttt{population/} (\texttt{eos.py}, \texttt{tov.py}, \texttt{fmode2.py}, \texttt{families.py}, \texttt{gen\_pop.py}, \texttt{gen\_base.py}, \texttt{paired\_analysis.py}, \texttt{families\_b.py}, \texttt{analytic\_benchmarks.py}, \texttt{convergence\_extra.py}, \texttt{dm\_prescription.py}, \texttt{prior\_width.py}, \texttt{robust\_report2.py}, \texttt{stat\_robust.py}, \texttt{cross\_family.py}, \texttt{frozen\_test.py}, \texttt{published\_tau\_comparison.py}, \texttt{bh\_qnm.py}, \texttt{robust\_report.py}), the derivation scripts (\texttt{derive\_vac2.py}, \texttt{derive\_matter.py}, \texttt{elim.py}), the experiment (\texttt{pop\_experiment.py}) and the report scripts. The code and data are on the \texttt{revised-analysis} branch of the repository below (the version described here is the release tagged \texttt{v2.0.0}, whose archived copy and DOI are to be given here before submission); the convergence tests are in \texttt{convergence\_tests.py}. The 360 generated EoS of the primary population (plus the additional records of the wide-prior sensitivity run) and all results, including those of the wide-prior run (\texttt{results\_pop\_*\_wide.*} and \texttt{wide\_prior\_results/}), are included as JSON/CSV; figures, tables and every number in the text are generated from them. Fixed seeds are used throughout. The environment is Python @@pyver@@ with scikit-learn @@skver@@. The released single-sequence data are at \url{https://github.com/wasif553/ML-NeutronStar-Classification}.

\appendix
\section{Single-sequence noise study (supplement)}\label{sec:legacynoise}
This appendix keeps the noise, oracle and uncertainty analysis performed on the single-sequence benchmark, with blocked validation. It is superseded by the population study for questions about composition, but it isolates the effect of measurement error on the original data and provides an exact oracle bound.

\subsection*{Mass regimes, errors, learners, oracle}
Three mass regimes are used: \textbf{A}, all models ($N=@@nTot@@$); \textbf{B}, $M\ge0.5\,\Msun$ ($N=@@nGe05@@$, with @@nDM05@@ dark-matter models); \textbf{C}, $M\ge1\,\Msun$ ($N=@@nGe1@@$, three classes). Contiguous mass blocks of six models are assigned at random to training (60\%), calibration (20\%) and test (20\%) roles; the analysis is repeated for 10 random assignments. Observables are perturbed as $x_j(1+s\sigma_j\epsilon_j)$ with $(\sigma_M,\sigma_R,\sigma_f,\sigma_\tau)=(5,5,2,20)\%$ at $s=1$. The learner is a three-member MLP ensemble, with logistic regression and a random forest as baselines. The oracle Bayes classifier knows the exact sequences (including the test blocks) and the error model,
\begin{equation}
P(k\mid\mathbf x^{\rm obs})\;\propto\;\frac1{N_k}\sum_{i\in k}\;\prod_j \mathcal N\!\left(x^{\rm obs}_j;\,x_{ij},\,(s\sigma_j x_{ij})^2\right),
\end{equation}
and is an upper bound for learners trained on finite data.

\subsection*{Results}
At $s=1$ in regime B the ensemble reaches @@accBSO1p0mlp@@\% with $\ftau$ against an oracle ceiling of @@accBSO1p0orc@@\% (chance 25\%); $M$ and $R$ alone give @@accBS1p0mlp@@\% (oracle @@accBS1p0orc@@\%). In regime C (three classes) the values are @@accCSO1p0mlp@@\% (ensemble) and @@accCSO1p0orc@@\% (oracle). Adding $f$ raises the oracle from @@accBS1p0orc@@\% to @@accBSF1p0orc@@\%; adding a 20\% $\tau$ gives @@accBSO1p0orc@@\%. Strange and hyperonic matter are the most confused pair (@@cmB1_13@@\% of strange models labelled hyperonic and @@cmB1_31@@\% the reverse). The raw ensemble is overconfident (ECE @@uB1p0rece@@, NLL @@uB1p0rnll@@ at $s=1$, regime B; oracle @@uB1p0oece@@, @@uB1p0onll@@); temperature scaling reduces them to @@uB1p0tece@@ and @@uB1p0tnll@@. Conformal sets have about 90\% marginal coverage and contain @@uB1p0tsetsize@@ of four classes at $s=1$ (oracle @@uB1p0osetsize@@). When the heaviest 25\% of each class is withheld, the ensemble reaches @@xSOexacc@@\% (oracle @@xSOorcacc@@\%) and the conformal sets cover only @@xSOexcov@@, since exchangeability fails; the ensemble's mutual information rises from @@xSOinmi@@ to @@xSOexmi@@ nats, an incomplete warning.
